WEBVTT

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.460
A new knitter recently
shared her goal sweater.

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.560
She told me that the hand knitting pattern

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:12.220
she wanted to use was
a slip stitch pattern.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.120
But looking closely at the photo,

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:20.660
it's a slip rib pattern incorporating
both knit and purl stitches.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:25.980
She has an LK150 knitting machine,
so we offered her some options.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:30.460
The hand knitting stitch pattern is written
row 1, knit 3,

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:38.060
slip one purl-wise, row 2, purl
across the row, and repeat these rows.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.380
We can translate this to machine knitting,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:46.620
keeping in mind that the purl stitches are
always facing us on our knitting machine.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:52.780
How about hand-manipulating every
fourth stitch to create that slip rib?

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:56.700
Here are two methods on a manual machine.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:03.400
First, knit about 10 rows and then
manually drop every fourth stitch.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.460
When relatching each column
of stitches, skip a row.

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.700
This will cause the stitch
to elongate and produce this result.

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:24.180
For the second method,
I left every fourth needle out of work

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:29.460
and then manually latched up the ladders,
again skipping every other row,

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.260
elongating in the knit
stitch on that purl background.

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:39.600
Please try both of these methods
to determine which one you prefer.

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:48.720
Dropping and relatching the stitches
created a looser and wider fabric.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.380
When keeping needles out of work,
the strands forming the ladders are

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:59.600
tighter, and the resulting fabric
is also tighter and narrower.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.700
If you have a ribber, you can make
short work of this stitch pattern.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.450
Set up your needles like this, having
every fourth needle on the main bed.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.940
This will create a three by one rib.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:17.380
Then set your main bed
to slip in one direction.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.920
This will form those elongated
stitch columns automatically.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.520
This technique can also be worked

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:29.890
on the ribber as Flat Rib.
Keeping all needles in work on one bed

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.640
and every fourth needle on the opposite
bed, slipping every other row.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.300
Slipped rib is a versatile technique
that can have lots of uses.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.640
Try it with your machine
and see which method you prefer.

